At new-task Plan entry, after identifying the request and active platform and completing the
workflow's task-control and shared-rule reads, read exactly
`.tfw/adapters/codex/coordinator.md` for the owner-facing startup card and initial mode choice.
At Plan Step 5 validate that choice and current capability; re-read this selected profile only
when the active surface or relevant capability materially changes. The task Coordinator uses the selected profile for new work. Do not preload other profiles or the tooling
manifest, and do not load this profile for every role command. A missing or ambiguous selected
pointer refuses a provider-specific offer and is reported without guessing.
For new work, explicitly select `manual`, `visible-agents` or `subagents`; all admit one or
several phases. One owner-facing Coordinator may own phase-local cycles under governing authority;
distinct bounded phase Coordinators remain available. Apply `conventions.md` → `Coordination modes`
for phase routes, independent context, continuation and actual capability limits. Historical GATEWAY/LEAD carriers remain readable,
but a principal, title or host never supplies a route or mandate.

### Codex native coordination

At new-task entry, inspect the task tools exposed in the current Codex task and report
`provision · addressed send · wait/readback · title/readback`, classifying each as `native`,
`owner-assisted`, or `unavailable`. `native` requires the corresponding mechanism for the selected mode: task creation or native
subagent spawn, exact addressed send, event-driven wait/status readback, and both title write plus
exact title readback for the final capability. A missing mechanism is owner-assisted or unavailable; capability
never grants authority or proves reliability.

A `/tfw-*` invocation activates work only when its exact skill, task/phase and owner-direct,
delegated or continuation source resolve. The command-only first message names the command and
task/phase; native origin and task files supply the source. Creating or selecting a task, assigning
a role prompt, sending a
briefing, waiting, or changing a title only provisions/navigates; none activates a workflow. Read and
validate the complete current task routing and selection fields before material work and report
every status change to the unit's `coordinator_route` under native-gates reporting. A newly launched
role receives only `/tfw-* <task[/phase]>` as its first message; its files supply context. Its
actual address may be recorded from the receipt or first normal gate, never invented in advance.

Before sending on the selected strict edges, apply `.tfw/conventions.md` → `Closed message
catalogue`: persist the owned record and required state/event, send only its permitted form, then
read the referenced record/current authority before the dependent act. Research keeps file-owned stage checkpoints and reference returns; owner–task Coordinator discussion stays substantive. The catalogue also owns the
one-time status fallback, delivery/duplicate handling and unavailable route.

For visible role tasks use cursor-based, event-driven `wait_threads` for readback/continuation;
for selected native subagents use their exposed event-driven collaboration wait/status. In either route, never call `read_thread`, request `includeOutputs`, or inspect raw global state,
session logs or unrelated task/chat history. After an unchanged signal, back off; never poll on a
short fixed timer or narrate unchanged state. A suspected stall permits one addressed status
request, then is reported as unavailable or blocked. Under `tfw-gates-only`, do not harvest or relay
another unit's unreturned reasoning, suspicions, prose or preferred solution.

When delegation is authorized, use distinct directly addressable role contexts in the selected
mode: user-visible Codex tasks for visible-agents, native subagents for subagents, or owner-started
sessions for manual. Record actual addresses/parents from receipts. For subagents use fresh context
(`fork_turns: none`), exact command-only activation, native collaboration addressed send/follow-up and
event-driven status/wait; no inherited owner/worker conversation or solution hints. Native completion
is no verdict. Apply the same closed reference catalogue on both transports. Mutating roles own
separate worktrees; independent review holds a reachable fixed Candidate. Preserve same-phase E/R
on corrections and apply the common new-phase context policy. A missing title/disposal control is
reported as that precise limit, never a reason to silently create another visible chat. Forked
history, relays and hidden helpers cannot supply role authority or independence.
Iterative dialogue requires an exact immutable two-peer
grant independent of task/phase topology. Fully
manual owner-transfer reporting requires its own explicit human selection.
